## It Happens on Well-Run Projects

Consider a project that has done everything correctly.

There is a contractually derived numbering standard. There is an approved revision procedure, issued and acknowledged. Every document moves through an enforced workflow in a common data environment. Transmittals are logged, sequenced, and reconciled monthly. The quality system passed its most recent surveillance audit with no findings against document control.

Eighteen months in, a supplier fabricates a skid to a superseded revision. The rework costs six weeks and a seven-figure sum.

The investigation finds no breach. The revision was issued correctly. The transmittal was sent to the right party on the right date. The receipt was logged. The vendor's document controller filed it. Nobody was careless, and no clause of the procedure was violated.

This is the observation that needs explaining, and negligence does not explain it. Nor does training, nor software, nor a stricter procedure — the project already had all three.

> **Key Takeaway:** If revision chaos only affected undisciplined projects, it would be a training problem. It affects disciplined ones, which makes it a design problem.

---

## Three Concepts, One Field

Most revision failures begin with a definitional collapse. Three distinct concepts are routinely recorded in a single field.

| | Question it answers | Changes when | Visible to | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Version** | Which iteration of the file is this? | Any save or edit | Originating team only | Author |
| **Revision** | Which issued state is this? | Formal issue | Every party | Document Control |
| **Status** | What may this be used for? | Review outcome | Every party | Approver / Client |

These are orthogonal. A single revision contains many versions. A single revision may carry different statuses at different points in its life.

```text
Rev B  ├─ v1  draft
       ├─ v2  internal check
       ├─ v3  discipline review
       └─ v4  →  ISSUED as Rev B, status IFR
       ←  returned Code 2
Rev C  ├─ v1  comment incorporation
       └─ v2  →  ISSUED as Rev C, status IFA
```

Collapse these into one field and the project loses the ability to state what a document is *for* — which is the only question that matters to the person about to act on it.

The collapse fails in two directions.

**Versions leak outward.** An engineer emails "v3, for information" to a vendor because a formal issue would take four days. That file is now in circulation with no revision identity, no status, and no transmittal. It will be quoted back to the project eight months later, and there will be no record that it was ever sent.

**Revisions do not bump.** A document is corrected and reissued under the same revision number, because "it was only a typo in the note block." Two different documents now share one identity. This is the most corrosive failure in the set, because every downstream control — transmittals, registers, approvals, audits — assumes that a revision number uniquely identifies a content state. Once that assumption is broken, the entire traceability chain silently stops working while continuing to appear intact.

One convention worth stating once: alpha revisions (A, B, C) before first approval, numeric (0, 1, 2) after, with the transition at first issue for construction. Conventions vary by sector and client. Whichever applies, it is not chosen by preference — it comes from the contract's identity rules, as covered in [Part 4](/engineering-information-governance/document-control-begins-at-the-contract/).

> **Key Takeaway:** A document can have many versions inside one revision, and one revision can hold different statuses over time. One field cannot carry three meanings.

