## Why the State Space Explodes

The reason revision chaos is universal on large projects is arithmetic, not behaviour.

Every issued revision creates copies, and every copy persists until something actively removes it. The population of document states in existence at any moment is approximately:

```text
States = Documents × Issued Revisions × Holders
```

Illustrative figures, not empirical:

| Project scale | Documents | Revisions | Parties | Total states | Current | Superseded |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Mid-size | 5,000 | 4 | 12 | 240,000 | 60,000 | 180,000 |
| Large EPC | 50,000 | 5 | 40 | 10,000,000 | 2,000,000 | 8,000,000 |

The ratio of superseded to current is `(R−1) : 1`. At five revisions, **four out of every five copies in existence are wrong.**

Two consequences follow, and both are counterintuitive.

**Correctness is the minority state.** Document control is not maintaining an orderly system against occasional disorder. It is holding back a growing majority. The default trajectory of an uncontrolled project is not drift — it is saturation.

**Parties multiply faster than documents.** Adding a subcontractor adds a full copy of the entire relevant population. This is why chaos scales with organisational count more aggressively than with document count, and why a 50,000-document project with eight parties is more controllable than a 15,000-document project with forty.

[Part 2](/engineering-information-governance/why-engineering-projects-lose-control/) described four parties holding four different revisions of the same drawing. That scenario is not an anecdote about a badly run project. It is the expected output of the arithmetic above, and it will occur on any project that does not actively counteract it.

> **Key Takeaway:** Revision chaos is not a deviation from the normal state. At scale it *is* the normal state, and control is what is imposed on top of it.

