## Some Concurrency Is Legitimate

The obvious response — permit only one live revision — is wrong, and projects that attempt it either fail or drive the concurrency underground.

Multiple live revisions are frequently correct:

| Situation | Revision in use | Revision in development | Why both are valid |
|---|---|---|---|
| Phased construction | C — IFC, Area 1 | D — IFR, Area 2 | Different physical scope, both live |
| Contractual freeze | C — client-approved | E — design progressed | Delta is a pending variation, not yet instructed |
| Vendor review cycle | 2 — approved | 3 — vendor internal | Not yet formally submitted |
| Long-lead procurement | B — ordered against | D — current design | Changing the order would trigger a claim |

In each case, suppressing the parallel revision would either stop work or conceal a commercial position. The concurrency is not a defect.

What makes it dangerous is when it is undeclared. A recipient holding Rev C needs to know whether it is current, frozen for contractual reasons, superseded for their area but valid for another, or simply out of date. That distinction is not carried by the revision number. It is carried by status.

Which is why the three-field separation in the previous section is load-bearing rather than pedantic: **status is the mechanism that makes legitimate concurrency safe.** Without it, every parallel revision looks identical to an error.

> **Key Takeaway:** The failure is not concurrency. It is undeclared concurrency.

