## Who Is Allowed to Create Rev D?

Revision authority is assumed to be self-evident. On multi-party projects it frequently is not.

| Document type | Where authority is ambiguous |
|---|---|
| Vendor document | The vendor revises to Rev 3; the EPC contractor adds a cover sheet and issues it as Rev C. One document, two identities, two revision sequences |
| Client-commented | Do client markups constitute a revision, or only the contractor's response to them? |
| Interface document | Two contractors on either side of a battery limit, both believing they hold the pen |
| Third-party certified | The certifying body reissues a certificate without notifying the contractor holding the register |

The rule is unambiguous even where practice is not: **exactly one party holds revision authority for any given document, and that party is named on the MDL.**

This extends [Part 3](/engineering-information-governance/why-mdl-is-the-most-important-document/)'s requirement that every register line names an individual rather than a department. Ownership of content and authority to change identity are different rights, and both need naming.

> **Key Takeaway:** Where two parties can both create Rev D, two Rev Ds will eventually exist — and no procedure will detect it, because both are procedurally correct.

