## If You Are Already Mid-Project

Most readers are not starting a project. Recovery is possible, but only forward — attempting to re-baseline history consumes more effort than it recovers. In order of cost, cheapest first:

1. **Separate the three fields before correcting any data.** Version, revision, status. Until they are structurally distinct, remediation writes better data into a structure that cannot hold it.
2. **Inventory live revisions and force a purpose declaration.** Every revision currently in circulation must state why it is live and for what. This is the fastest correction available and requires no system change.
3. **Build a controlled-copy register from today forward.** Do not attempt retrospective reconstruction of who holds what. Register new issues, and let the historical population age out.
4. **Convert distribution to pull for new issues only.** Leave historical transmittals untouched. Changing distribution mode retroactively creates more confusion than it resolves.
5. **Audit identity collisions.** Same revision number, different content. This is the only item on the list worth a full sweep — the count will be small, the consequences are disproportionate, and each instance quietly invalidates every downstream record that references it.

> **Key Takeaway:** Do not attempt to re-baseline history. Contain the forward population and let the superseded one age out.

