## Conclusion

Return to the project in the first section. The procedure was not breached. The procedure was written for a state space it does not address.

```text
Documents × Revisions × Parties
            ↓
Superseded copies always outnumber current ones
            ↓
Control is containment, not prevention
```

A revision procedure describes how a document moves from one issued state to the next. It says nothing about the several hundred thousand copies of previous states already distributed across forty organisations — which is where the failure actually occurs.

Two questions are frequently treated as one:

> *"Is this document current?"* is a revision question.
>
> *"Are we all working from the same reality?"* is a governance question.

Revision control answers the first reliably. Only the mechanisms in this article — single issue point, revision as identity, pull distribution, explicit supersession, declared purpose, controlled copies, escalated withdrawal — begin to answer the second.

The projects that stay coherent at scale are not the ones with stricter procedures. They are the ones that accepted the arithmetic and designed against it.

